Yes. Since you want a fully white-labeled, customized enterprise build, I would structure the whole process as a source → development environment → customization → build → packaging → validation → signed release pipeline.

One important correction from earlier: the current Hermes repository says its recommended contributor workflow is actually to use the standard installer to create the managed checkout and then work from that checkout; it also provides a manual clone/venv path. Since you specifically want maximum control over your customized fork, the manual path is reasonable. 

1. Create your private fork

Fork/clone the upstream repository:

git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent.git
cd hermes-agent

Then create your own private repository and push your customized branch there.

Upstream Hermes
      ↓
Your private fork
      ↓
Your Enterprise Hermes


---

2. Install the development prerequisites

Install only the tools needed for your build machine:

Git
uv
Node.js

Hermes currently uses Python 3.11 for its development environment, and its repository includes Node workspaces/components. 

You do not run:

iex (irm https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/install.ps1)

for this workflow.


---

3. Create a separate development venv

Hermes specifically recommends keeping the development venv outside the source checkout. 

For example:

uv venv C:\EnterpriseHermes\venvs\dev --python 3.11

Activate it:

C:\EnterpriseHermes\venvs\dev\Scripts\activate


---

4. Install Hermes into that development environment

From your cloned repository:

uv pip install -e ".[all,dev]"

This is an editable development installation.

Meaning:

Your source code
       ↓
Development venv
       ↓
Hermes

When you modify the source, the development environment sees your changes.

Hermes' current contributor documentation uses this same uv pip install -e ".[all,dev]" approach. 


---

5. Install the Node dependencies

From the repository:

npm install

The current repository uses npm workspaces and includes Node-based components such as the browser tooling/TUI/web/desktop workspaces. 


---

6. Now do your white-label customization

This is where you make it your product.

Branding

Change:

Hermes
Nous Research
hermes
Hermes Agent

where appropriate to your product identity.

For example:

Hermes Agent
       ↓
Acme AI Agent

and:

hermes
       ↓
acme-agent

CLI

Instead of:

hermes --help

your final CLI can become:

acme-agent --help

Paths

Change application/config/data paths where appropriate:

~/.hermes

to your own application namespace.

UI/TUI/Desktop

Change:

Product name

Logo

Icons

Titles

About screen

Version information

Startup messages

Help text

Documentation links

URLs

Error messages where they expose branding



---

7. Modify the functionality

Now make your actual product changes:

Core Agent
   │
   ├── Guardrails
   ├── Security controls
   ├── Custom behavior
   └── Enterprise configuration

Skills
   │
   ├── Remove unwanted skills
   ├── Modify existing skills
   └── Add your own skills

Tools
   │
   ├── Add/remove tools
   └── Modify tool behavior

Don't modify the installed venv for these changes.

Modify the source repository.


---

8. Lock your dependencies

Once your customized version works, lock the dependency state.

Hermes' development guidance recommends maintaining dependency bounds and regenerating uv.lock when dependencies change. 

Conceptually:

pyproject.toml
      +
uv.lock
      ↓
Exact reproducible build

This is important for enterprise releases because you don't want:

Build 1 → dependency version X
Build 2 → dependency version Y

without intentionally changing your product.


---

9. Test the customized Hermes

Run the project's tests:

scripts\run_tests.sh

On Windows, use the appropriate test invocation if the shell script isn't directly usable.

Also test:

CLI
Agent execution
Tools
Skills
LLM providers
Memory
Scheduling
Gateway
TUI/Desktop components you retain

And run:

your-agent doctor

after you have renamed the CLI.


---

10. Create a clean build environment

This is a very important step.

Don't package your messy development machine.

Create a clean build machine/environment:

Clean Windows Build Machine
│
├── Git
├── Python/build tooling
├── uv
├── Node build tooling
└── Your private source

Then:

git checkout <release-commit>
uv sync
npm ci

Build from that exact source commit.


---

11. Build the Python application

Now package your customized Python application.

The conceptual transformation is:

Your Python source
       +
Python 3.11 runtime
       +
Python dependencies
       ↓
Python application binary/package

You can evaluate:

Nuitka

Source
  ↓
Nuitka
  ↓
Native executable + dependencies

PyInstaller

Source
  ↓
PyInstaller
  ↓
Executable + embedded Python + dependencies

For a serious enterprise product, I would benchmark both on Hermes rather than deciding purely theoretically, because Hermes has a fairly large and dynamic dependency/tool surface.


---

12. Bundle Node.js

Do not make your final product depend on:

User's Node.js

Instead:

YourProduct/
    runtime/
        node/
            node.exe

Your application launches that specific Node executable when Node functionality is required.

The current Hermes repository declares Node >=20, while the official installer currently provisions Node 22. 

For your enterprise build, pin the exact Node version you test and ship.


---

13. Bundle other required runtimes

Depending on which Hermes functionality you retain, package the required supporting binaries/resources too.

The official Hermes installer currently handles dependencies including:

Python
Node.js
ripgrep
ffmpeg

automatically. 

Your enterprise package should therefore deliberately account for every runtime dependency rather than assuming the customer's machine has it.


---

14. Create your final application layout

For example:

AcmeAgent/
│
├── AcmeAgent.exe
│
├── runtime/
│   ├── node/
│   │   └── node.exe
│   │
│   ├── ffmpeg/
│   └── ripgrep/
│
├── resources/
│
├── skills/
│
├── templates/
│
└── config/

The exact layout depends on what you package versus keep external.


---

15. Create the Windows installer

Instead of giving users a ZIP containing your development environment, create:

AcmeAgentSetup.exe

The installer should:

Install
  ↓
Application files
  ↓
Private runtimes
  ↓
Required resources
  ↓
Shortcuts
  ↓
File associations/configuration if required
  ↓
Your CLI


---

16. Test on a completely clean machine

This is your most important deployment test.

Create a Windows VM with:

Python       ❌
Node.js      ❌
uv           ❌
Hermes       ❌
Git          ❌
ffmpeg       ❌
ripgrep      ❌

Install only:

AcmeAgentSetup.exe

Then verify:

AcmeAgent.exe
AcmeAgent --help
AcmeAgent

and every functionality you intend to ship.

If it works there, you have demonstrated that your package is genuinely self-contained.


---

17. Security/enterprise validation

Before release:

Source
 ↓
SAST
 ↓
Dependency/CVE scan
 ↓
Secret scanning
 ↓
License scanning
 ↓
Malware scanning
 ↓
SBOM
 ↓
Build
 ↓
Binary scanning
 ↓
Code signing

Also maintain a record of the exact:

Git commit
Python version
Python dependency versions
Node version
Node dependency versions
Other bundled binaries
Build tool version


---

18. Sign the final installer

Finally:

AcmeAgentSetup.exe
        ↓
Code signing certificate
        ↓
Signed enterprise installer

Then distribute that signed artifact.


